{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.BoundedMeromorphicLiouville","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.BoundedMeromorphicLiouville","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Meromorphic.NormalForm","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Meromorphic.Order","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.Basic"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.BoundedMeromorphicLiouville.entire_toMeromorphicNFOn_of_bounded","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.BoundedMeromorphicLiouville.entire_toMeromorphicNFOn_of_bounded","kind":"theorem","line":76,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/BoundedMeromorphicLiouville.lean#L76-L85","doc":"**Bounded ⇒ the normal-form representative is entire.** For any `f`, if its\nnormal-form representative `g = toMeromorphicNFOn f univ` is bounded, then `g` is\nentire. This is the boundedness analogue of brick (c)\n(`RatioEntire.entire_toMeromorphicNFOn_div_of_same_order`): there the ratio is made\nentire by matching divisors; here, by a uniform growth bound (the state-integral\nendowment). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.BoundedMeromorphicLiouville.entire_of_meromorphicNFOn_bounded","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.BoundedMeromorphicLiouville.entire_of_meromorphicNFOn_bounded","kind":"theorem","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/BoundedMeromorphicLiouville.lean#L66-L74","doc":"**Bounded meromorphic-normal-form ⇒ entire.** A function in meromorphic normal\nform on `ℂ` that is bounded has nonnegative order everywhere (no poles), and a\nnormal-form function of nonnegative order is analytic — so it is entire. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.BoundedMeromorphicLiouville.meromorphicOrderAt_nonneg_of_bounded","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.BoundedMeromorphicLiouville.meromorphicOrderAt_nonneg_of_bounded","kind":"theorem","line":41,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/BoundedMeromorphicLiouville.lean#L41-L64","doc":"**Bounded ⇒ meromorphic order `≥ 0` everywhere.** If `‖f z‖ ≤ C` for all `z`,\nthen `f` has no pole: a negative-order point would force `f → ∞`\n(`tendsto_cobounded_of_meromorphicOrderAt_neg`), impossible under a uniform bound\n(the punctured neighbourhood `𝓝[≠] z` is `NeBot` in `ℂ`, so the empty preimage of the\nunbounded target cannot lie in it). ","signatureChunk":0}]}